{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2307.03198, how do the European Commission's AI HLEG and Google's AI policy each address fairness and accountability in AI governance?", "answer": "The AI HLEG emphasizes fairness and accountability as core values, while Google highlights addressing fairness, bias, and accountability in its AI policy.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "Similar values were highlighted by the European Commission's AI HLEG, including human dignity and autonomy, prevention of harm, fairness, explicability, accountability, privacy, and social and environmental well-being (AI HLEG 2019)."}, {"page": 5, "quote": "Key values that inform Google's position include empowering humanity, uplifting society, and addressing fairness and bias. Concern for privacy and accountability are also mentioned: Google states that it will not use surveillance that violates internationally accepted norms, nor will it pursue weapons or systems intended principally to cause harm."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2307.03198, what is the relationship between virtue ethics and ethics-based auditing for trustworthy AI?", "answer": "Virtue ethics nurtures individual values, while ethics-based auditing or internal review processes are recommended to complement virtue ethics for trustworthy AI.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "Such investments in training in virtue ethics complemented by ethics-based auditing or internal review processes appear to be promising avenues to build trustworthy AI within the proposed multilevel framework."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2307.03198, what are the three dimensions of trust in people, and how are they mapped to trust in technology and automation?", "answer": "The three dimensions are competence/ability, integrity, and benevolence; these map to functionality, reliability, and helpfulness in technology, and to performance, process, and purpose in automation.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "| Competence/ability                                            | Functionality                                                      | Performance                                         |\n| Integrity                                                     | Reliability                                                        | Process                                             |\n| Benevolence                                                   | Helpfulness                                                        | Purpose                                             |"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2307.03198, what is the definition of AI literacy provided by Long and Magerko?", "answer": "AI literacy is defined as a set of competencies enabling individuals to critically evaluate, communicate with, and use AI as a tool in various contexts.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "AI literacy has been defined as \"a set of competencies that enables individuals to critically evaluate AI technologies; communicate and collaborate effectively with AI; and use AI as a tool online, at home, and in the workplace\" (Long and Magerko 2020, 598)."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2307.03198, which international panel was modeled after the IPCC to support responsible AI, and what values does it promote?", "answer": "The International Panel on Artificial Intelligence (IPAI) was modeled after the IPCC and promotes human rights, inclusion, diversity, and innovation.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "The International Panel on Artificial Intelligence (IPAI) at the Group of Seven (G7) summit in 2019 aimed to follow the example of the International Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) to support the responsible development of AI \"grounded in human rights, inclusion, diversity, and innovation\" (Government of Canada 2019)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2307.03198, which U.S. state law is mentioned as emulating the EU's GDPR, and what aspect does it focus on?", "answer": "The California Consumer Protection Act (CCPA) is mentioned as emulating the GDPR, focusing on data privacy.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "One related legislation, however, is the California Consumer Protection Act (CCPA), which focuses on data privacy and generally emulates the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) enacted by the EU (Somers and Boghaert 2018)."}]}
]}
